{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793499239,"nonce":"WikPRLb3tlaZ6Abeu3AHTN7+FXI=","title":"Every module compile rebinds interpreters over everything loaded: quadratic in load() count","message":"compile_module_entered builds an InterpreterBinding after each module, including each Lua load() chunk. Building it copied every loaded function into a fresh map (binding_parts), listed every loaded global's address under the Cranelift lock (interpreter_globals), and collected every function id as a tick; serving it dropped the previous binding's copies; binding the runtime interpreter registered a tick callback for every function and rebound every global, and HirInterpreter::bind_global_slot rescans every cached function's constants per global. The closure builders (body_source, frame_exit_hook, address_source, bridge) each copied another map over all functions per bind.\n\nofficial/constructs.lua joins about 6800 chunks. Release, Mac: interpreter_binding was 54% of the per-load time and set_lazy_interpreter (dropping the previous binding) 20%. Debug x86_64 under Rosetta (CI's native tests (x86_64-apple-darwin) job): constructs.lua passes 480 s no longer and timed out in Rust CI 41256809880 (plain and warm-up). The base 9f18acb1 has the same profile (binding 51% plus serving 8% early in the run), so the cost predates that commit; the job had not reached the Lua tests since 9f18acb1 because the OSR test failed first.","files":null}]}